<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Val di Chiana</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Valdichiana</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Val di Chiana is a broad valley in eastern Tuscany and western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/umbria">Umbria</a>, running from near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arezzo">Arezzo</a> south to Chiusi and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-trasimeno">Lake Trasimeno</a>. In Etruscan times its fertile soils supported Clusium, Cortona and Arretium and linked central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/etruria">Etruria</a> with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tiber-river">Tiber</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arno">Arno</a> valleys. It later turned to marsh and was drained by the grand dukes of Tuscany between the 16th and 19th centuries.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/val-di-chiana.jpg" alt="Val di Chiana" width="320" height="212" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: PMM · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Valdichiana_panorama.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
